Original Zero picture decoder regression and independent review

Run zero-picture-check without arguments. It builds fixtures in memory in
original Zero and invokes the actual shared decoder; no fixture files, foreign
codec or subprocess is needed. Recompose with:
  zero-integrate PROJECT NEW_CHECK.zero --picture-check
Its decoder region must exactly match media/zpix-decoder.zero.part. The native
checker uses 46 variables and a 67112960-byte workspace.

Completed 2026-11-17:
Linux memory checker: 42/42 passed.
Windows PE memory checker under Wine: 42/42 passed.
Independent native CLI alias/publication checks: 5/5 passed.

36 malformed cases vary input bounds, signature, zero/oversized/full-u32
dimensions, channels, encoding, pixel/payload/total counts and their high words,
checksum, reserved fields, zero/excessive runs, changed RGB, short raster and
an extra record after completion. Six valid cases cover binary RGB, runs255/256,
1080p, 4K uniform and worst-case 4K alternating pixels. Every successful RGB
pixel is compared with an independently constructed expectation. Every case
checks unchanged input hash and output prefix/suffix guard values.

No unresolved correctness defect was found in the reviewed native pipeline.
The reviewed fragment includes the builder's explicit upper-word/input-cap
guards for 32-bit reuse. These checks executed on native64, not an actual32 VM.
Exact four-byte records bound input loads. count<=pixels and run<=pixels-count
prevent count wrap and decoded overrun. Maximum decoded memory is 24883264
bytes including its64-byte prefix. Worst-case encoded length is33177664,
below its33554432-byte region. CLI input/output regions are disjoint; the
reusable decoder explicitly requires disjoint caller-owned buffers.

The complete RGB FNV checksum is checked before any destination open. It is a
corruption checksum, not authentication. Invalid input may dirty the private
output buffer; callers must not consume it. CLI writes are exclusive and use
complete writes, flush and close, with failed-new-output cleanup. They are not
an atomic publication guarantee against crashes/concurrent filesystem edits.

Independent probes confirmed source==destination, hardlinked output and symlink
output are refused without changing source contents. A bad checksum neither
creates a missing destination nor modifies an existing one. Its syscall trace
contains only the input open and exit65, with no destination open/create.

External evidence:
